text:
Protecting the main branch
What it is. A setting on GitHub that makes every change reach the main branch through a pull request. (The main branch is the one the project treats as current; GitHub calls it the default branch, and its name varies from project to project.) The plugin never renames it unless the maintainer asks.
Why. Each change is visible before it lands. That includes changes made by an AI agent working through a person’s GitHub account, which GitHub treats as that person.
Who does it. The maintainer, once per repository. Setting up a project with the plugin includes this as a step for the maintainer. Agents do not change repository settings; they describe the steps for the person who can.
What the plugin’s steps say. The maintainer creates a branch ruleset on GitHub (Settings → Rules → Rulesets → New ruleset → New branch ruleset) that:

is named (for example “Protect default branch”) and set to Active;
targets the default branch;
restricts deletions, blocks force pushes, and requires a pull request before merging;
requires as many approvals as the team wants. A maintainer working alone uses 0, because GitHub never lets the author of a pull request approve it;
leaves the bypass list empty unless the team decides otherwise. Anyone on that list can push straight to the branch, and so can any agent acting through their account.

A maintainer who works in R can do the same from the R console. The plugin gives a short check that reads the repository’s settings and reports whether the branch is protected, and the matching command. The person runs these; agents only suggest them. The check can be rerun at any time, for example after the repository moves to a new owner.
A limit. On the free GitHub plan, a private repository cannot use rulesets. The plugin assumes a public repository and says so if someone plans a private one.

R/setup.R
What it is. The script every other script relies on. It attaches the packages the analysis uses and defines the shared values.
What’s in it.

every library() call for the analysis;
named constants: any number a reader might want to change (a time window, a year, a threshold, a ratio) gets a name here instead of appearing as a bare number in a later script;
code lists (the diagnosis or procedure codes that define a condition);
shared paths and parameters.

The labels on the participant flow diagram are also built from these constants, so the diagram can’t fall out of step with them.
What it never holds. The location of restricted data. That is found only through the data source’s own package configuration.
Rules for the other scripts. They don’t call library(), don’t run setup.R again, and don’t hard-code values.

Writing readable R
The code is written so that a clinician-analyst who learned R from online guides can read it. In practice:

data steps use pipes and the dplyr and tidyr verbs; case_when() replaces nested if;
repeating a step over subgroups, years or models is a plain loop over names, or a list looked up by name;
clever shortcuts are avoided, such as the apply family, Reduce or picking items by position ([[1]]);
where base R is unavoidable, it sits inside a named, tested function with a comment saying why.

A plain step beats a clever one. A project’s own established conventions win over these. Old code is brought into line only in a style-only pull request, which changes no logic and shows that every model and table is identical before and after.

What the next person finds
You. At the end, the assistant tells you what was recorded where. It offers a short message for the PI or the team pointing to it. Nothing is sent as you without your go-ahead.
The assistant. When someone later takes over, their assistant starts from the repository: the analysis plan, open decision issues, open pull requests and their notes, and anything you marked as paused. It does not rely on your files or chats.
In the repository. What the next person finds there:

your drafts and notes, as draft pull requests, issue comments or issues;
each open item finished, handed on, closed with a reason, or paused with what it waits on;
notes on open pull requests: what remains, the merge order and any conflicts;
an issue for each unanswered request;
if ownership moved, the repository under its new owner.

Who decides. The deciders named in AGENTS.md, as before. If no one picks the work up, the project can stay dormant, with its open items and their reasons on record.
More. Joining.

Written for version 0.1.14. Part of Contributing in your role, which lists the sources.
You. The maintainer owns or administers the repository. That can be anyone on the project, and a PI may also be named as a decider and use the plugin directly. Some steps on GitHub need admin rights, so they fall to whoever has them.
The assistant. It describes the one-off GitHub steps and leaves them to you, because agents don’t change repository settings:

protecting the main branch so changes arrive only through pull requests;
turning on GitHub Pages so the website goes live;
tags, for example when the plan is locked, which are made by the maintainer or with their explicit say-so;
accepting the repository if a leaving owner transfers it to you, then updating the site’s links and turning Pages on again;
removing anything pushed by mistake from the history. Agents don’t attempt this.

If you work in R, it can suggest the console commands one at a time, after checking where things stand. You run them; it never does. Like everyone, you can ask for a periodic check-in that lists what is waiting on you: assigned issues, decisions you make, and pull requests awaiting your review.
In the repository. Branch protection, the Pages setting and tags live on GitHub rather than in files. AGENTS.md records the deciders, the data sources and what agents may do with each.
Who decides. You settle the areas AGENTS.md assigns you. Allowing agents to do more with a data source is a change a person makes to AGENTS.md through a pull request, naming the agreement that permits it. Agents never propose it.
More. On GitHub, not in files, The plan and its decisions, Start a project.

Joining
The assistant starts from what you want to contribute and what you already know, and gives one next step at a time. It explains the project through its own documents:

the website, the way in for anyone who doesn’t code: a clinician starts at Clinical background and Study design and methods;
the analysis plan, which says what the analysis must do, with markers for questions still open;
GitHub issues, which hold those open questions and known gaps;
AGENTS.md, which says how work is done here and who decides what;
ARCHITECTURE.md, for people who will work on the code.

Ways to contribute:

Clinical input without code: comment on a decision issue with evidence and reasoning, or review the plan or the site. Only the named deciders settle a question, but anyone can inform one. A decider who doesn’t use GitHub can decide by email; their exact words are then posted in the issue.
Code: a change on a branch, offered as a pull request. The assistant explains only the Git steps your own tool needs.
Review: anyone can ask for one (see Review).

It also offers your own ChatGPT or Claude project for the repository, with instructions for how you like to work (review only, step by step, or hands-off), and an optional check-in that reports what is waiting on you.

What happens
The assistant asks a short set of questions, one at a time, and reuses anything you have already told it or that is already in the files. It does not ask you to install R or Git unless the work needs them; a clinician can help with the protocol from the browser.
Then it proposes a setup plan and, once you agree, drafts:

the analysis plan (analysis-plan.md), from a template that fits the design (observational, economic or descriptive), with a decision issue for every scientific choice still open rather than a guess;
AGENTS.md, the project’s rules for any assistant: who the deciders are and for what, every data source and the package that reads it, and what an assistant may never read;
the website starter, once you’ve said whether the site will be published from a folder in the repository or by a GitHub workflow;
a README, a reference list, and a .gitignore that keeps data and secrets out of the repository;
steps for the maintainer to protect the main branch, so every change arrives through a pull request, and to switch on the website.

It also offers, without requiring them: your own ChatGPT or Claude project for this repository with instructions filled in for how you like to work, milestones with dates, and a periodic check-in that tells you what is waiting on you.

What happens
Each definitive script is divided into labelled sections. Its page on the website shows those sections, imported from the script itself, so the code on the page is always the code that runs. Under each section the page explains, in plain language for a clinician:

what the step does and why;
what it takes in and where that comes from;
which part of the analysis plan it carries out;
what it hands on to the next step.

Abbreviations are defined, associations are not described as causes, and sources are cited from the project’s reference list. Where code and plan disagree, the page says so rather than smoothing it over.
The page and the sidebar are updated in the same pull request as the code change. The rendered site is either updated in the same pull request or published by a workflow, as the project chose.

Who it is for
Anyone working on such a project:

clinicians who will never write R, and who contribute through the plan, the website and the discussion of open questions;
people who lead the work (students, residents, fellows or faculty) and may know some R but not GitHub, or neither, often for a few weeks before handing it on;
statisticians, who review methods and decide statistical questions;
the person who maintains the repository, often the principal investigator.

The plugin does not assume which of these you are. It asks what you want to do and how much help you want.
